vm-10 — ship a byte-compiled prelude boot image (fast startup)  [plan-2, follow-up to vm-9]

WHAT
  A build step compiles bootstrap/prelude.lisp -> a byte-code fasl loaded at boot instead of
  re-reading + re-macroexpanding the ~11k-line source every start.  The MECHANISM already
  exists after vm-8/vm-9: COMPILE-FILE emits a byte-code fasl (DEFUN bodies pre-compiled),
  LOAD installs the templates with no recompilation, and the COMPILE-FILE fallback now
  survives a per-form control-stack overflow (falls back to interpreted for that form).
  What remains is the BUILD + BOOT wiring and the validation.

WHY IT WAS DEFERRED OUT OF vm-9 (measured, not assumed)
  1. Uniform compilation is NOT a uniform win.  vm-9 microbenchmarks (COMPILE'd vs
     tree-walked, same source):
        tight arithmetic loop (sumsq 2e6)   ~9.8x   FASTER   <- the VM sweet spot
        naive fib 28 / tak                  ~1.0x   (call-bound; hot calls are to BUILT-INS)
        push-in-a-loop (revbuild 3e5)       ~0.7x   SLOWER   <- a tight cons loop; cons is a
                                                     BUILT-IN, so each call builds an arg list
                                                     + kernel_apply.
     So compiling the WHOLE prelude could SLOW DOWN call-bound prelude paths.  UPDATE
     (v1.8.19): fast-apply is now implemented, but it did NOT fix these — it only skips the
     arg list for COMPILED callees, and the hot calls here are to BUILT-INS (`+ - < ` /
     `cons`), which still build an arg list.  The actual fix is vm-inline-primitives.issue
     (execute the hot arithmetic/cons/car/cdr/compare primitives as byte-code ops).  vm-10
     should land ON TOP of inline-primitives, or compile SELECTIVELY (hot arithmetic/loop
     functions only), not blanket-compile.
  2. Cost/robustness of a build-time whole-prelude compile: compiling all ~972 prelude
     functions in one process took >10 min and can overflow on individual deeply-nested
     forms (now survived by the SERIOUS-CONDITION fallback, but each fallback loses the win).
  3. Whole-file compilation SEMANTICS: the prelude relies on compile-time side effects
     (defmacro / eval-when / in-package ordering).  Booting from a compiled image must
     reproduce them exactly; needs its own validation pass against the full 42-suite.

PLAN
  - Prereq (or concurrent): vm-inline-primitives so compiled call-/alloc-bound code is >=
    the tree-walker, removing the revbuild-style regression.  (fast-apply, v1.8.19, is done
    but insufficient on its own — see above.)
  - Add a `make` target: boot a stage-0 bocl, (compile-file "prelude.lisp") -> prelude.cfasl.
  - main.c: prefer prelude.cfasl over prelude.lisp when present + version-compatible
    (BYTECODE_VERSION / CFASL_VERSION check); --no-compiled-prelude to force source.
  - VALIDATE: full 42-suite green booted from the compiled image; measure startup delta and
    an end-to-end (require :asdf) compile+load+OPERATE vs the ~240-300s original + plan-1.

STATUS OF THE UNDERLYING PIECES (all DONE, on branch vm): COMPILE / COMPILE-FILE emit
byte-code with a safe SERIOUS-CONDITION fallback; LOAD installs templates name-preservingly;
tests/test_misc.c::test_vm_selfhost compiles a feature-spanning corpus (recursion, closures,
iteration+multiple-values, conditions) to a byte-code fasl and runs it green.  This ticket is
only the fast-startup packaging + selective/fast-apply strategy + boot wiring.

DEPENDS ON: vm-9 (done), fast-apply (vm-6b deferred).

CLOSE-OUT (v1.8.27, branch vm)
  DONE — byte-compiled prelude boot image shipped.
  - make target: `make -C sources prelude-image` -> bootstrap/prelude.cfasl
    (778 fns byte-compiled; ~10 min build; gitignored generated artifact).
  - main.c prefers a sibling prelude.cfasl when present + cfasl/byte-code
    version-compatible (bocl_fasl_load returns NIL on mismatch -> source
    fallback, never a half-image); --no-compiled-prelude and $BOCL_PRELUDE_IMAGE
    override.
  - COMPILE-FILE reworked for the bootstrap semantics concern (#3 above): the
    whole-file MACROEXPAND-ALL is dropped (it was slow, stack-overflowed, and
    baked the fully-booted compiler's macros into forms the boot had not reached
    — e.g. a doc'd DEFPARAMETER's (SETF (DOCUMENTATION ...)) before that
    setf-expander exists).  Now only DEFUNs are expanded (to BOCL:DEFINE-FUNCTION
    for the byte-compile hook); every other form is kept RAW so LOAD expands it
    in boot order, exactly like a source boot.  Blanket-compile (not selective)
    turned out fine once this was fixed.
  - Two boot-order defects fixed: SET-SYMBOL-FUNCTION now accepts a
    COMPILED-CLOSURE (DEFGENERIC installs one); the prelude sibling loader keys
    on the last "/" not the literal "prelude.lisp".
  VALIDATION: full 42-suite green (source boot, shared kernel/prelude changes);
  image boot behaviourally identical to source boot across a broad feature
  exercise; startup ~3.17s -> ~1.07s (~3x).  New regression: test_misc
  "vm compiled prelude".
